# TODO - Native Linux packaging

## Objective

Produce portable Linux x86-64 executables with local asset discovery.

## Acceptance

- Do not link or bundle original game executables or libraries.
- Verify startup, save paths, packaging and Linux gameplay smoke tests.
- Verify shared persistent display and input settings in native gameplay:
  resizable aspect-fit, integer scaling, fill/crop, stretch and fullscreen.
- Verify resize, high-DPI and touch-coordinate mapping across display modes.
- Verify remappable per-player keyboard, controller and touch profiles.

## Evidence

The selected-title release build produced an 835,768-byte dynamically
linked x86-64 ELF executable on the development host. Its direct dynamic
dependencies were SDL2, libgcc and libc; it does not use the original game
executable or library. The hidden native four-player movement smoke passed
with four verified local art bundles. This verifies local startup, not
portable distribution, independent asset discovery, saves or complete
gameplay. Those remain open.

The native `package TITLE OUTPUT` command now compiles the admitted
title, preflights bounded executable and licence sources, and writes
them into a new private directory. It rejects an existing destination,
unknown title, wrong ELF target and unreviewed dependency licence
metadata. Original media, imported canonical assets and system
libraries are not bundled. Publication now uses private sibling staging,
synchronises files and directories, and performs a no-replace rename.
A failed write leaves no final package; a post-rename parent sync error
is reported separately.

The first local package contained a 984,632-byte x86-64 ELF and 59
non-executable files. Its recorded SHA-256 matched independent hashing.
Reinvocation at the same destination was rejected. From the package
directory, the hidden touch, pose, enemy and dummy-audio smoke passed
using explicitly selected external bundles. The package remains local
to the development host. Linux ABI portability, save paths and complete
gameplay remain open.

The native launch catalogue now resolves explicitly pinned local asset
identities from an executable-adjacent file or `--config FILE`. Paths
are relative to the catalogue. Both forms passed the complete hidden
native smoke from an unrelated working directory using a newly built
package and external assets. Duplicate keys and a symlinked catalogue
were rejected. The catalogue is a trusted local selection, not media
scanning or an authenticity proof. Portable distribution and save
paths remain open.

A fresh package after the atomic publication change contains a
1,013,360-byte executable whose independently calculated SHA-256
matches its receipt. Its complete hidden native smoke passed with the
adjacent local catalogue from an unrelated working directory.
